  "coach.play": "Playing {point}.",
  "coach.fallback": "I am a rule-based coach, not a general AI, so I may have missed how you phrased that. Try one of the buttons below, or ask about a Go term, the rules or the position.",
  "lesson-help.board": "Each stone sits on a crossing of two lines, so a 9×9 board has 81 points. Black always moves first. Stones stay where they are put unless they are captured, and a player may pass instead of playing.",
  "lesson-help.liberties": "Count liberties by looking up, down, left and right of a stone. A stone in the middle has 4, on an edge 3, in a corner 2. Connected stones share their liberties, and the number matters because a group with none is captured.",
  "lesson-help.capture": "To capture, fill the last empty point beside the enemy stone. The white stone already has black stones on three sides, so the one green dot is the capture point.",
  "lesson-help.groups": "Two stones that touch along a line are one group. The white pair shares its liberties, and only one is left, at the bottom. Fill it and the whole group comes off the board at once.",
  "lesson-help.atari": "The black stone has white stones on three sides and one liberty left. If you do nothing, White plays there and captures it. Extending to that point joins your new stone to the old one and gives the group more liberties, so it is no longer in atari.",
  "lesson-help.suicide": "At A all four neighbours are white stones, so a black stone there would have no liberties. It would also capture nothing, because each white stone still has liberties of its own. A move like that is suicide and is not allowed.",
  "lesson-help.exception": "This time the white stone at the top has black stones on three sides, so A is its last liberty. Playing at A fills that liberty and captures the stone first. Once it is removed, your new stone has a liberty, so the move is legal.",
  "lesson-help.ko": "In the starting position Black can capture the white stone by playing A. That leaves one black stone with a single liberty, and White could capture it straight back at B, then Black could capture again, for ever. The ko rule stops that: White must play somewhere else first.",
  "lesson-help.eyes": "A and B are two separate empty points, each completely surrounded by black stones. If White plays at A, the stone has no liberties, and Black still has the other eye at B, so Black is not captured and the white move is suicide. The same is true at B. White could only fill an eye if the other one were already gone, which is why two eyes make a group safe.",
  "lesson-help.scoring": "Each side scores its stones plus the empty points that only it surrounds. Black has 9 stones and the 27 empty points on its left; White has 9 stones and the 36 points on its right, so White is ahead by 9 before komi.",
  "lesson-help.strategy": "Corners are efficient because two edges help your stones, sides come next, and the centre is hardest to hold. Early on, play on the third and fourth lines.",
  "lesson-help.connect": "Stones are connected only if they touch along a line, up, down, left or right. Two stones that meet at a corner (diagonally) can be cut: White plays at A or B and splits them into two weaker groups. Playing at A or B joins your stones into one group that shares its liberties.",
  "lesson-help.cut": "The two black stones touch only at a corner, so the points A and B are both cutting points. A white stone on either one stands between them. Black can still connect with the other point, but the cut forces Black to spend a move on it, and that is how cuts win tempo and make groups weak.",
  "lesson-help.ending": "A player may pass instead of playing. Two passes in a row end the game. Before counting, dead stones (stones that could not survive) are removed, then each side adds its stones to the empty points only it surrounds. White also adds komi.",
  "lesson-help.handicap": "Handicap stones go on the star points, so a weaker player can take Black with an advantage. White then moves first and komi is reduced to half a point. On a 9×9 board two stones go on opposite corners, three add a third corner, and so on up to five.",
  "lesson-help.ask-point": "Ask me about a particular point, or press Hint.",
  "lesson-help.strategy-where": "Try one of the starred points: C3, G3, C7 or G7. They are on the third line near a corner, which balances territory and safety.",
